From 1892880d46b9103c24917363b3bf1861e9297ff7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:41:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] drm/asahi: g17: let a refused render take back idle
 render pools

Every queue that renders has a render pool whose pages are mapped in an
address window that the pools of all clients share; the window has room
for about forty pools. A render that finds no room for its queue's pool
first asks idle queues to give theirs back, and only then waits: a queue
with nothing in flight, whose pool the firmware has released, populates
its pool again on its next render.

That request never returned a render pool. The reclaim hands the pages
it takes back to the caller's batch, which frees them after the device
lock is released, and it stops when the batch has no room; both callers
passed a batch created without room for render pool pages. Until now a
render pool was given back only when its queue was destroyed, or by the
event worker after its whole client had been idle and evicted under
memory pressure. A client that kept more queues with populated pools
than the window holds therefore got no room for one more, although the
others were idle: the render waited about 26 seconds and failed, and
the client saw a lost device after

  G17 capacity refusal LowerVa count=...
  GPU work of a client failed (EBUSY, ...)

Give the reclaim of both capacity waits a batch with room for the pools
it gives back, and run it only while the window is short: while it
refuses a pool, or has no room left for one more. At other times a
refusal of other capacity takes no pool back. On M5 nothing changes.

More queues rendering at the same time than the window has room for
still wait, for up to about 26 seconds, until one has been idle long
enough for the firmware to release its pool.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
(cherry picked from commit 5c25150ed6d8f563d2eb49bcde2e79d8749df27a)
